Experiment 5: String Operations in Shell Script & C
A comprehensive technical manual analyzing dual-paradigm string manipulation in Linux, comparing high-level Bash parameter expansion against pointer arithmetic in C, and benchmarking execution profiles via the time utility.
EXPERIMENT5
String Length and Substring Extraction in Shell Script and C
1.  Aim
To write a shell script and an equivalent C program that each:
find the length of a string entered by the user;
extract a substring from that string, given a starting position and a number of characters;
and then compare the execution time of the two programs using the time command.


4.  Part A — Shell Script (exp7.sh)
4.1  Program
#!/bin/bash

read -p "Enter a string: " str

echo "Length of string: ${#str}"

read -p "Enter starting position: " start

read -p "Enter number of characters: " length

echo "Substring: ${str:$start:$length}"


4.2  Line-by-line Explanation
Step 1 — The shebang
#!/bin/bash


This first line tells Linux which interpreter should run the file — in this case, the Bash shell.
Step 2 — Taking the string as input
read -p "Enter a string: " str
read — takes input from the user.
-p — displays a prompt before reading.
"Enter a string: " — the prompt message shown on screen.
str — the variable in which the input is stored.
So if the user types hello world, then str="hello world".




Step 3 — Finding the length
echo "Length of string: ${#str}"
The important part here is ${#str}:
# — asks Bash for the length.
str — the variable being measured.


In other words, it returns the number of characters stored in str.
Input string
Length
hello
5
hello world
11


Step 4 — Reading the starting position
read -p "Enter starting position: " start
If the user enters 2, then start=2.


Step 5 — Reading how many characters are needed
read -p "Enter number of characters: " length
If the user enters 5, then length=5.


Step 6 — Extracting the substring
echo "Substring: ${str:$start:$length}"


The general syntax of this parameter expansion is:
${ string : start : length }
 │   │       │       │
 │   │       │       └── Number of characters to extract
 │   │       └────────── Starting position
 │   └────────────────── Variable containing the string
 └────────────────────── Parameter expansion
It means: start from position $start and take $length characters.


For example:
Variable
Value
str
hello world
start
2
length
5
Result
llo w


