6.  Comparing the Execution Time
Run each program with the time command placed before it:
time ./exp7
time ./exp7.sh


6.1  Reading the Output of time
The output generally looks like this:
real    0m0.005s
user    0m0.001s
sys     0m0.004s


Field
Meaning
real
Total elapsed (wall-clock) time from start to finish
user
CPU time spent executing user-level code
sys
CPU time spent in kernel/system calls


1. real
real    0m0.005s
Real time = actual elapsed time from the moment your program starts until it finishes.
Here:
0.005 seconds = 5 milliseconds
Think of it as a stopwatch:
START ───────────────► END
          0.005 sec
2. user
user    0m0.001s
This is the amount of CPU time spent executing your program's code in user space.
Here:
0.001 seconds = 1 millisecond
For example, calculations, loops, string operations, etc. generally contribute to user CPU time.
3. sys
sys     0m0.004s
This is the amount of CPU time the program spent in the Linux kernel on its behalf.
For example, operations involving system calls such as:
read
write
open
close
can contribute to sys time.
Here:
0.004 seconds = 4 milliseconds
Will it remain the same?
No. It can change every time you run the program.
This happens because your computer is doing other things at the same time, and factors such as CPU scheduling, cache state, disk/terminal I/O, VM overhead, and system load can affect timing.
For your Ubuntu VirtualBox
Since you're running Ubuntu inside VirtualBox, small variations are especially normal because the VM is sharing your physical CPU/resources with the host system.
